<p>The U.S. military said it struck a vessel in the eastern Pacific Ocean on Friday, killing two men and leaving one survivor, in the latest attack on a boat it alleged was involved in drug trafficking. U.S. Southern Command said Joint Task Force Southern Spear carried out the strike at the direction of Gen. Francis L. Donovan against a vessel it said was operated by “Designated Terrorist Organizations”. SOUTHCOM said it notified the U.S. Coast Guard to begin search-and-rescue operations for the survivor and reported no U.S. military injuries. The strike is part of a campaign launched by President Trump’s administration in September against vessels Washington describes as carrying narcotics from Latin America through the Caribbean and eastern Pacific.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Srlk4GBQDjKfU753Y38f0r5XUrfHQDcCwFFnPUyZOPDoCAe5ixZxUneo3v0K5DVa57nOX14FD9mNfquYyag1gWTgA-Hqf4gP4AhDZn4iqfnVGpdErjCkt9GTIkafQn65bAnmIMsc42ilfd92StZEMKrVnnI' alt='Image published with coverage of the U.S. strike on an alleged drug-trafficking boat in the eastern Pacific.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Campaign toll: The latest deaths brought the reported toll from U.S. boat strikes in the Caribbean and eastern Pacific to at least 189 by AFP’s count and more than 190 in other tallies since September.</li><li>Recent pace: The New York Times described Friday’s attack as the third boat strike in five days, with 57 attacks reported in the Caribbean and eastern Pacific against boats accused of drug trafficking.</li><li>Search lead: A U.S. official cited by The New York Times said the Mexican Navy was leading the search for the survivor.</li><li>Immediate-threat issue: Legal experts and rights advocates questioned the legal basis for strikes on crews who were not shown in the cited reports to pose an immediate threat to the United States.</li></ul><blockquote>unsubstantiated, fear-mongering claims - American Civil Liberties Union</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. Southern Command: SOUTHCOM said designated terrorist organizations operated the targeted vessel and that intelligence confirmed it was on known narcotics-trafficking routes. (<a href='https://www.foxnews.com/us/us-military-kills-two-alleged-narco-terrorists-lethal-strike-drug-vessel-eastern-pacific'>Fox News</a>)</li><li>Human Rights Watch and Amnesty International: The rights groups described the maritime strikes as “unlawful extrajudicial killings,” challenging Washington’s use of lethal military force against suspected traffickers. (<a href='https://www.brecorder.com/news/40420359/us-military-says-its-strike-on-vessel-in-eastern-pacific-kills-2-leaves-1-survivor'>Business Recorder</a>)</li><li>American Civil Liberties Union: The ACLU challenged the administration’s descriptions of targeted crews, arguing that officials had not substantiated the claims used to justify the strikes. (<a href='https://www.brecorder.com/news/40420359/us-military-says-its-strike-on-vessel-in-eastern-pacific-kills-2-leaves-1-survivor'>Business Recorder</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/us-military-says-it-killed-2-in-strike-on-alleged-drug-trafficking-boat'>US military says it killed 2 in strike on alleged drug-trafficking boat</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/may/08/us-military-strike-eastern-pacific'>US military strike on vessel in eastern Pacific kills two people, leaving one survivor</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/two-killed-as-us-military-strikes-on-alleged-drug-boat-in-eastern-pacific/article70958014.ece'>Two killed as U.S. military strikes on alleged drug boat in eastern Pacific</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/one-survivor-strike-alleged-drug-boat-eastern-pacific-kills-2/'>Pentagon says 1 survivor after latest strike on alleged drug boat kills 2</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/US/wire